{
    "title": "Amendments to CFTC Rules of Practice and Rules Relating to Investigations",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 03, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-03/pdf/2025-21888.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register features three housekeeping updates across aviation, market enforcement, and highways, all meant to clarify rules, streamline processes, and retire outdated requirements.\n\nFirst, the Federal Aviation Administration is removing a slice of controlled airspace above Carter Ranch Airport in Oakwood, Texas. This follows the cancellation of instrument flight procedures at that airport, which means the extra protected airspace is no longer needed. Pilots and chart makers may see simpler maps and fewer restrictions in that area. On the other hand, pilots who relied on instrument procedures there will have fewer low-visibility options, which could reduce accessibility in bad weather.\n\nSecond, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission is updating how it handles enforcement matters to improve transparency and completeness. The agency clarified that it can accept settlements by issuing an order with findings and penalties, and it set clearer requirements for the staff memo that recommends a settlement, including laying out the facts, law, and even unfavorable points. The agency also updated its process for notifying people who may face an enforcement action: when notice is given, it should be in writing or promptly confirmed in writing, and those people will generally have at least 30 days to respond, up from 14. These changes should benefit companies and individuals by making the process clearer and fairer, and they should help the agency make well-documented decisions. A possible downside is that added steps and longer timelines could slow some cases, and staff may face more paperwork.\n\nThird, the Federal Highway Administration is rescinding a 1974 rule about bridges built on federal dams. The agency says the rule simply repeats what the law already requires and that no funds have been available for this type of project for years. Removing the rule cleans up the code without changing current practice. States and federal agencies still must follow the statute if such projects are ever funded again. Some may worry that scrapping regulations could weaken oversight, but in this case the practical effect appears minimal.\n\nOverall, the likely winners are stakeholders who value clearer, more current rules: pilots and airspace users who want charts to match actual needs, market participants who want transparent enforcement, and transportation officials who prefer a simpler rulebook. Those who might feel downsides are pilots losing instrument options at a specific airport, enforcement teams facing added process requirements, and readers who prefer to keep older rules on the books even if they are duplicative.",
